{"id":"h051","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"A string 100 centimeters long has a mass of 4 grams and emits a frequency of 250 vibrations per second. Another string 100 centimeter long has a mass of 1 gram. What is the frequency of the second string if it is put under the same tension as the first?\nIs the correct answer \"500vps\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"500 vps is the correct answer, assuming both strings vibrate in the fundamental mode.","confidence":0.93,"lean":1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.0},{"text":"For a string fixed at both ends, the fundamental frequency is f = (1/2L)√(T/μ).","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Both strings have the same length and tension, so the only difference is the mass per unit length.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The second string's linear density is one quarter of the first's.","confidence":0.98,"lean":1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Frequency scales as 1/√μ, so the second string's frequency is 250 × 2 = 500 vps.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The problem does not state that 250 vps is the fundamental or that both strings vibrate in the same mode, so a different mode would change the answer.","confidence":0.4,"lean":-1,"theta":0.6283,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Real-world effects such as stiffness, damping, and end conditions are ignored but are normally neglected in textbook problems.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The standard reading of the problem is the fundamental mode, so the unstated-mode issue is only a matter of interpretation.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J